	affiliations = {College of Fisheries, Guangdong Ocean University, Zhanjiang, 524088, China; Museum of Aquatic Organisms, Guangdong Ocean University, Zhanjiang, 524088, China},
	abstract = {To evaluate the impact of artificial planting of mangroves on the macrofauna and ecological quality in coastal wetlands, a survey of macrofauna was conducted on the artificially planted mangrove wetland in Longzi Village, eastern Leizhou Peninsula, Zhanjiang in spring (April) and summer (August) of 2024. The diversity and community structure characteristics of macrofauna were analyzed, and multiple index methods were applied to evaluate the benthic ecological quality. The results showed that a total of 37 species of macrofauna were recorded in the survey area, with the highest number of mollusks (21 species, 56.76%), followed by annelids (8 species, 21.62%) and arthropods (7 species, 18.92%), and only one species was found in other groups. The average density and biomass of macrofauna in spring were (340±160) ind./m<sup>2</sup> and (161.02±83.06) g/m<sup>2</sup>, respectively, which were higher than the (313±223) ind./m<sup>2</sup> and (131.22± 97.19) g/m<sup>2</sup> in summer, respectively. In terms of diversity, the Shannon diversity index (H'), Simpson diversity index (D), and Pielou evenness index (J') in spring were all higher than those in summer. In terms of tidal areas, the above diversity indices showed the pattern: low tide area>mid tide area>high tide area. The results of nMDS (non-metric multidimensional scaling) analysis showed that the macrofaunal communities in different seasons were clustered into separate groups, but there was no significant difference (PREMANOVA, P>0.05). The macrofaunal communities in different tidal areas were separated from each other and there were significant differences between each pair (PREMANOVA, P<0.05). The evaluation results of the ABC curve showed that the abundance and biomass accumulation curves of macrofaunal communities in spring and summer both intersect, indicating that benthic ecological quality were subject to certain disturbances. Both AMBI and M-AMBI index results showed that the overall benthic ecological quality is good in spring and summer, with all stations being in a“good”or“excellent”state. However, an obvious tidal zone gradient was also observed, i. e., the benthic ecological quality in low tide zone>mid tide zone>high tide zone. This study can provide scientific basis for the construction of the“Mangrove City”, the restoration of mangrove ecosystem functions, and ecological protection in Zhanjiang. © 2025 Chinese Society for Oceanology and Limnology. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {AMBI; biotic index; ecological quality assessment; macrobenthos; mangroves},
