	abstract = {We used environmental DNA (eDNA) metabarcoding and bottom trawl surveys to assess fish community diversity and structure in the Yellow Sea. eDNA metabarcoding detected significantly higher species richness (86 vs. 41 species), alpha diversity (Shannon, Simpson, and Chao1 indices), and taxonomic/phylogenetic/functional richness than bottom trawling. The PCoA results revealed clearer geographic clustering in eDNA data, while the RDA analysis identified temperature, NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>, and NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> as key environmental drivers for both methods. eDNA captured more biodiversity components and local functional richness, demonstrating its potential to complement trawling for efficient, non-invasive coastal ecosystem monitoring. © 2025 by the authors.},
