	affiliations = {J. J. Strossmayer University, Department of Biology, Cara Hadrijana 8/A, Osijek, Croatia; University of Kragujevac, Faculty of Science, Institute of Biology and Ecology, Radoja Domanovića 12, Kragujevac, 34000, Serbia},
	abstract = {The Western Balkans is an important hotspot of earthworm diversity, yet remains a critical data gap in European soil biodiversity assessments. We compiled 2801 earthworm occurrences from 1202 locations in Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Serbia, and Montenegro (1900–2024 period). The dataset includes 118 species from 18 genera. Species richness ranged from 1 to 15 (median = 2). Sampling completeness reached 76.6 % (Chao2 estimate: 154 ± 20 species). However, notable gaps persist in wetlands (52.2 %) and grasslands (52.5 %) compared to forests (88.2 %). Spatial autocorrelation of species richness was negligible (Moran's I = 0.051, p = 0.001), suggesting that environmental gradients, rather than geographic proximity, drive observed diversity patterns. Endemic species were concentrated in the Dfb (warm-summer humid continental) climate zone and montane forests (Dinaric and Balkan mixed forests: >35 % endemics). Rarefaction analyses showed that initial habitat-related differences in community composition (PERMANOVA: F = 1.94, p = 0.001) were artifacts of unequal sampling (3–620 sites per habitat). Environmental variables such as altitude, soil pH, and clay content significantly influenced ecological groups: epigeic species were associated with higher elevations and acidic, low-clay soils, while endogeic species prevailed in deeper, structured soils. Of the 58 endemic species (49.15 % of all taxa), 40 (69.0 %) were recorded within protected areas, including 18 in national parks, while 18 (31.0 %) occurred only outside protected zones. These findings highlight both the conservation value of protected areas and the need to address gaps. The dataset provides a robust baseline for future research and supports integrating earthworms into biodiversity policy. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
