	affiliations = {Department of Zoology, Vimala College, Thrissur, Calicut University, India; Department of Zoology, St. Alberts College (Autonomous), Kerala, Kochi, 682018, India},
	abstract = {The environmental health of marine ecosystems is largely determined by benthic communities, which are integral to ecosystem function. A large number of indices have been developed to assess benthic ecosystems. Here we provide the comprehensive synthesis of benthic quality assessment challenges within the Indian context, emphasizing region-specific methodological inconsistencies. Amongst the various indices, the Marine Biotic Index and the Multivariate Marine Biotic Index are the most frequently used indices in Indian coastal ecosystems. The adoption of many specialized metrics such as the Benthic Index, the Benthic Opportunistic Polychaetes Amphipoda Index, and the Benthic Opportunistic Annelida Amphipods Index is limited. To understand why, we critically analyse the methodological challenges associated with benthic assessments, including taxonomic uncertainties, limited biodiversity baselines, and monsoon-driven variability. We proposes an interdisciplinary pathway to strengthen benthic monitoring, and necessity of standardized protocols, capacity building, and multidisciplinary integration to enhance the reliability and applicability of benthic ecosystem assessments in India. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
