	affiliations = {Engineering Technology Research Center of Henan Province for Aquatic Animal Cultivation, College of Fisheries, Henan Normal University, Xinxiang, 453007, China; Observation and Research Station On Water Ecosystem in Danjiangkou Reservoir of Henan Province, Nanyang, 473000, China; The National Ecological Quality Comprehensive Monitoring Station (Hebi Station), Hebi, 458000, China},
	abstract = {Land use change caused by human activities has become one of the main drivers of global biodiversity loss. The middle reaches of the Yellow River (MYR) play a crucial ecological role in the Yellow River basin. The region is characterized by complex topography, variable hydrological conditions, and intense human disturbance, making it a typical ecologically fragile area. Currently, there is limited research on the effects of different land use types on macroinvertebrate community structure and diversity in such fragile ecosystems. This study set up a total of 33 sampling points in the middle reaches of the Yellow River from May to July 2021 and systematically evaluated the impacts of different land use types on the taxonomic and functional diversity of macroinvertebrate communities. The PERMANOVA analysis shows significant spatial differences of macroinvertebrate community structure existed among different land use types. Forest land exhibits significantly higher taxonomic and functional α diversity indices compared to agricultural and urbanization land (p < 0.05). Taxonomic and functional β diversity indices across different land use types are primarily driven by species turnover. The RDA results indicate that the LUI has the most significant impact on the biodiversity of macroinvertebrates. These findings highlight the significant effects of different land use types on the biodiversity of macroinvertebrates in the MYR area. © 2025 The Author(s)},
