	pages = {3575 - 3597},
	doi = {10.1007/s10750-025-05830-4},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105000010606&doi=10.1007%2Fs10750-025-05830-4&partnerID=40&md5=1e660871e2133d65b30050ac9cd32243},
	affiliations = {Centro de Estudos do Mar, Universidade Federal do Paraná, Avenida Beira Mar, Paraná, Pontal do Paraná, Zip Code 83255-976, Brazil; Departamento de Química, Pontifícia Universidade Católica do Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil; Molecular Ecology Group (MEG), Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerca (CNR), Istituto di Ricerca Sulle Acque (IRSA), Rome, Italy},
	abstract = {Most of the beach biodiversity consists of small animals that are inconspicuous and live in the spaces between sand grains. These animals play a crucial role in shaping interactions within beach ecosystems and are important for understanding how these ecosystems respond to both natural and human-induced disturbances. In tropical and subtropical regions across the globe, members of Saccocirridae dominate the interstitial fauna, with different species preferring areas with different morphodynamic states. Abundant and ubiquitous, saccocirrids likely drive beach ecosystem functioning, yet the lack of information on their dietary preferences obscures our understanding of their specific trophic role in those ecosystems. In this study, we investigate the use of fatty acid analysis to infer potential dietary sources of two species of interstitial annelids from the Saccocirridae family in Brazil: Pharyngocirrus gabriellae, which is found in protected embayed beaches; and Saccocirrus pussicus, which prefers exposed reflective beaches. We collected up to 1000 saccocirrid individuals from intertidal sediments across 12 Brazilian beaches spanning from Santa Catarina to Rio de Janeiro. We quantified 34 different fatty acids from each sample using gas chromatography/mass spectrometry, and the resulting values were analysed to infer likely dietary sources based on proportions of specific fatty acids referenced in the literature. Our multivariate regression analysis indicated that both species of saccocirrids likely feed on similar carbon sources and revealed a relationship between the fatty acid profile and primary production, mean grain size, and salinity of each sampled beach. Whereas fatty acid analysis provides valuable insights into potential dietary sources, further research is required to conclusively determine the dietary preferences of the group. These findings address the ‘Raunkiaeran’ shortfall by incorporating dietary data about two saccocirrids species, which are valuable traits for understanding the ecological role of these worms in coastal sandy shores. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.},
