	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105022727947&doi=10.1016%2Fj.rsma.2025.104615&partnerID=40&md5=bae2f651513f6c9e59fc548f083153a5},
	affiliations = {Marine Ecology Laboratory, Department of Life Sciences, Presidency University, 86/1, College Street, Kolkata, 700073, India; ESSO-National Centre for Polar and Ocean Research, Ministry of Earth Sciences, Headland Sada, Goa, Vasco-da-Gama, 403804, India},
	abstract = {Macrobenthic recruitment is a critical process that underpins the structure and functioning of benthic ecosystems. This study delves into the different life cycle stages of macrobenthic communities from two high Arctic fjords, Kongsfjorden and Krossfjorden, Svalbard. Outer fjords and open ocean zones, characterized by higher total organic carbon (TOC) and fine-grained silty sediments, supported diverse assemblages dominated by polychaetes. Early settlers, including juvenile polychaetes, molluscs, and crustaceans, were especially abundant in these regions. Meroplankton communities were primarily composed of crustacean larvae, particularly Cirripedia. Hierarchical Modelling of Species Communities (HMSC) indicated that environmental variables, particularly sediment texture, TOC% and surface chlorophyll a , explained maximum variance proportion in taxon abundances. Sand% in sediment was positively correlated with adult molluscs and arthropods, while TOC% was crucial for early settlers and adult annelids, sipunculids. The model’s high explanatory power (R² = 0.883) and robust fit demonstrate the significant influence of environmental factors in shaping Arctic fjord benthic communities. These results underscore the importance of ecological modelling in integrating macrobenthic life stages (larval, early settlers, and adult) which offers a cohesive framework to unravel settlement dynamics and the environmental processes shaping macrobenthic community structure in Arctic fjords. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
