	affiliations = {Sharjah Marine Science Research Centre, University of Khorfakhan, United Arab Emirates; Norwegian Institute for Water Research, Region South, Grimstad, NO-4879, Norway; Department of Benthic Ecology, Alfred Wegener Institute Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research, Am Handelshafen 12, Bremerhaven, 27570 , Germany; Ifremer, Unité COAST Laboratoire Environnement et Ressource Bretagne Nord, 38 Rue Du Port Blanc, Dinard, 35800, France; Hafok AB, Stenhamra, SE 179 61, Sweden; Office Francais de la Biodiversite. Station Marine de Dinard, Centre de Recherche et d'Enseignement Sur les Systèmes Côtiers (CRESCO), 38 Rue du Port Blanc, Dinard, 35800, France; Faculty of Oceanography and Geography, University of Gdańsk, Al. Marsz. Pilsudskiego 46, Gdynia, 81-378, Poland; Laboratoire d'Ecogéochimie des Environnements Benthiques (LECOB), Observatoire Océanologique de Banyuls, CNRS, Sorbonne Université, UMR 8222, Banyuls-sur-Mer, 66650, France; National Research Council of Italy, Institute of Anthropic Impacts and Sustainability in the Marine Environment (CNR-IAS), Loc. Sa Mardini, Torregrande, Oristano, 09170 , Italy; Faculty of Biosciences and Aquaculture, Nord University, Universitetsalléen 11, Bodø, 8026 , Norway; Deep-Sea Ecology and Technology Department, Alfred Wegener Institute Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research, Am Handelshafen 12, Bremerhaven, 27570 , Germany; Department of Natural Sciences, University of Agder, Centre for Coastal Research-CCR, Kristiansand, 422 4604, Norway; Station de Recherches Sous-Marines et Océanographiques (STARESO), Pointe Revellata, Corse, 20260, France; Dept for Marine Research, Senckenberg am Meer, Wilhelmshaven, Germany; Institute for Chemistry and Biology of the Marine Environment, Univ. of Oldenburg, Wilhelmshaven, Germany; Royal Belgian Institute of Natural Sciences, Operational Directorate Natural Environment, Marine Ecology and Management (MARECO), Vautierstraat 29, Brussels, 1000, Belgium; Wageningen University, Wageningen Marine Research, P.O. Box 77, Yerseke, 4400, AB, Netherlands},
	abstract = {Benthic organisms are important ecological receptors, playing fundamental roles across seafloor ecosystems, delivering some of the most important functions in the marine environment. Some of these key benthic functions include nutrient cycling, food provision for higher trophic levels, and carbon storage. Over the past 6 years, benthic monitoring has faced growing complexity, driven by diminishing funding and the constraints imposed by the COVID-19 pandemic. These challenges underscore the pressing need to recognize the enduring value of benthic time series in supporting monitoring, management, and modelling efforts. These long-term data sets have been critical to advance our current understanding into the areas of cumulative effects, conservation, management of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs), development of indicators, and assessment of climate-driven changes in marine ecosystems. Ongoing expert group discussions consistently affirm both the relevance and necessity of continuing to collect these vital data sets. However, the focus on emerging technologies and so-called 'cutting-edge' approaches sometimes leads to the undervaluation and compromising some of these long-term series. We contend that a comprehensive understanding of benthic ecology, essential for robust marine management, reliable numerical analysis, and taxonomic consistency, cannot be achieved without the continuity provided by long-term data. Such time series are indispensable for tracking patterns of change and assessing responses across diverse human activities and seafloor ecosystems. While our research has concentrated on soft sediment environments, many of the key principles and recommendations outlined here are broadly applicable to other ecosystem types.  © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of International Council for the Exploration of the Sea.},
	author_keywords = {and Directives; benthos; collaboration; eDNA; management; monitoring; seafloor; time series},
