	affiliations = {Laboratory of Biodiversity, Parasitology and Ecology of Aquatic Ecosytems, Faculty of Sciences of Tunis, University of Tunis El Manar, Tunis, 2092, Tunisia; University of Carthage, Faculty of Sciences of Bizerte, Zarzouna, 7021, Tunisia; Department of Biology, College of Science, Princess Nourah bint Abdulrahman University, P.O. Box 84428, Riyadh, 11671, Saudi Arabia; LR01ES14 Laboratory of Environment Biomonitoring, Coastal Ecology and Ecotoxicology Unit, Faculty of Sciences of Bizerte, University of Carthage, Zarzouna, 7021, Tunisia; Biology Department, College of Science, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh, 11623, Saudi Arabia},
	abstract = {The Mullet (Chelon auratus) is a eurythermal and euryhaline fish, with high protein content in muscles and eggs. In the current investigations, 40 individuals were examined for the prevalence of parasites. Approximately half of the investigated individuals were infested with three parasites, comprising two copepods (Lernanthropsis mugilis, prevalence P = 25 %, mean intensity M.I = 1.4 and abundance A = 0.7 and Parabrachiella mugilis, P = 17.5 %, M.I = 1.95 and A = 0.7) and Myxobolus exiguus (P = 15 %, M.I = 1.83 and A = 0.55) respectively. The infested fish showed also significant changes in the activity of four biochemical markers, namely catalase, glutathione S-transferase, acetylcholinesterase, and malondialdehyde content, respectively, in gills, liver, and muscles. The results of the current investigations suggest that the parasite L. mugilis was more aggressive compared with P. mugilis, whereas M. exiguus was more prevalent on the gills of mullets, where the most significant changes in biomarkers were recorded. © 2024},
