	affiliations = {Senckenberg Museum für Naturkunde Görlitz, Abteilung Bodenzoologie, Görlitz, 02806, Germany; Thünen-Institut für Biodiversität, Bundesallee 65, Braunschweig, 38116, Germany; School of Forestry, University of Canterbury, Christchurch, 8140, New Zealand},
	abstract = {Soil biodiversity is characterised by an enormous richness of species and individuals representing diverse groups of organisms. The soil habitat consists of various sub-habitats or compartments. Soil organisms are usually classified into several size classes that are adapted to different soil compartments. The spectrum ranges from small organisms that are only visible under the microscope to large animals such as earthworms. In addition, soil organisms are organised in complex food webs, different species having different feeding strategies and representing various trophic levels. In particular, the interactions of species within these food webs drive a large number of soil processes and thus form the basis of many ecosystem services such as nutrient cycles for the plant growth that is essential for life on earth including humans. © 2024 W. Kohlhammer, Stuttgart.},
