	affiliations = {Dipartimento di Scienze e Tecnologie Biologiche e Ambientali (DiSTeBA), Università del Salento, Lecce, 73100, Italy; Hanse Wissenschaftskolleg – Institute for Advanced Study, Delmenhorst, 27753, Germany; Instituto de Ciências Do Mar, LABOMAR, Universidade Federal do Ceará, Fortaleza, 60165-081, Brazil; CoNISMa, Consorzio Nazionale Interuniversitario per le Scienze del Mare, Rome, 00196, Italy; Departament de Biologia Evolutiva, Ecologia i Ciències Ambientals, Universitat de Barcelona, Barcelona, 08028, Spain; Institut de Recerca de La Biodiversitat (IRBio), Universitat de Barcelona, Barcelona, 08028, Spain; Department of Ocean Systems, NIOZ Royal Netherlands Institute for Sea Research, Texel, 1790AB, Netherlands; Bentho-Pelagic Processes, Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research, Bremerhaven, 27570, Germany; Laboratorio di Spettrometria di Massa Analitica e Isotopica, Dipartimento di Beni Culturali, Università del Salento, Lecce, 73100, Italy; Ocean BioGeosciences, National Oceanography Centre, Southampton, S014 3ZH, United Kingdom; MARUM – Center for Marine Environmental Sciences, University of Bremen, Bremen, 28359, Germany; Senckenberg Am Meer, Marine Research Department, Wilhelmshaven, 26382, Germany; NBFC, National Biodiversity Future Center, Palermo, 90133, Italy; Instituto Español de Oceanografía, Centro Oceanográfico de Gijón, (IEO-CSIC), Gijón, 33212, Spain},
	abstract = {Cold-water coral (CWC) reefs of the Angolan margin (SE Atlantic) are dominated by Desmophyllum pertusum and support a diverse community of associated fauna, despite hypoxic conditions. In this study, we use carbon and nitrogen stable isotope analyses (δ<sup>13</sup>C and δ<sup>15</sup>N) to decipher the trophic network of this relatively unknown CWC province. Although fresh phytodetritus is available to the reef, δ<sup>15</sup>N signatures indicate that CWCs (12.90 ± 1.00 ‰) sit two trophic levels above Suspended Particulate Organic Matter (SPOM) (4.23 ± 1.64 ‰) suggesting that CWCs are highly reliant on an intermediate food source, which may be zooplankton. Echinoderms and the polychaete Eunice norvegica occupy the same trophic guild, with high δ<sup>13</sup>C signatures (-14.00 ± 1.08 ‰) pointing to a predatory feeding behavior on CWCs and sponges, although detrital feeding on <sup>13</sup>C enriched particles might also be important for this group. Sponges presented the highest δ<sup>15</sup>N values (20.20 ± 1.87 ‰), which could be due to the role of the sponge holobiont and bacterial food in driving intense nitrogen cycling processes in sponges’ tissue, helping to cope with the hypoxic conditions of the reef. Our study provides first insights to understand trophic interactions of CWC reefs under low-oxygen conditions. © 2023, The Author(s).},
	correspondence_address = {B. Vinha; Dipartimento di Scienze e Tecnologie Biologiche e Ambientali (DiSTeBA), Università del Salento, Lecce, 73100, Italy; email: beatriz.vinha@studenti.unisalento.it},
