	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85094894675&doi=10.1007%2Fs41208-020-00263-3&partnerID=40&md5=2c31b40ba3b2be267106ab21d9133e1b},
	affiliations = {Health and Environment Laboratory, Faculty of Sciences Ain Chock, University Hassan II, B.P. 5366 Maârif, Casablanca, 20100, Morocco; Prospection of Littoral Resources Laboratory, National Institute for Fisheries Research (INRH), Road Sidi Abderrahmane Club Equestre Ould Jmel, Casablanca, Morocco; Centre for Advanced Studies of Blanes (CEAB-CSIC), Accés a la Cala St. Francesc, 14 Blanes, Girona, 17300, Spain; Specialized Center in Zootechny and Marine Aquaculture Engineering, National Institute of Fisheries Research, BP. 31, M’diq, 93200, Morocco; UMR 6554 LETG-Nantes, University of Nantes, BP 81227, Nantes, 44312, France},
	abstract = {The paper analyses the composition, structure and spatial organization of the soft-bottom macrozoobenthos inhabiting Oualidia lagoon (Moroccan Atlantic coasts), as well as their relationships with the main environmental variables. Material for the study was collected from 43 stations in winter 2013. A total of 56 taxa belonging to 6 phyla were recorded. Species diversity showed the highest values for molluscs, crustaceans and polychaetes. We used a Hierarchical Ascending Classification and a non-Metric Multidimensional Scaling to characterize the macrozoobenthos of the lagoon. We identified three groups of stations according to the outer, middle and inner part of the lagoon, hosting arranged from the outer -most to inner-most areas of the lagoon. The trophic structure of the macrozoobenthos was dominated in density by deposit-feeders (88.1%) and species richness by carnivores (20%) and detritivorous (20%). The BIO-ENV analysis showed that the combination of granulometry, salinity and temperature were the major factors controlling the spatial distribution of the macrozoobenthos in the Oualidia Lagoon. © 2020, Springer Nature Switzerland AG.},
