	affiliations = {Portuguese Institute for the Sea and Atmosphere (IPMA), Avenida 5 de Outubro, Olhão, 8700-305, Portugal; Center of Marine Sciences (CCMAR), University of Algarve (UAlg), Campus de Gambelas, Faro, 8005-139, Portugal; Testa & Cunhas, Fishing and Aquaculture, Avenida Marginal, s/n, Gafanha da Nazaré, 3830-552, Portugal},
	abstract = {The epifauna associated to farmed mussels in southern Portugal coast was analysed, aiming at identifying the species with spreading potential through commercial transport. The presence of a relevant number of the species here found is not reported to at least one of the common mussel export/transposition countries. Indeed, important species biogeographic dissimilarities between the mussel farm area and the Greater North Sea and Western Mediterranean Sea sub-regions were detected, suggesting the potential transport of non-indigenous species (NIS) into other countries. Among them, fouling species such as the anemones Paractinia striata and Urticina felina, the acorn barnacles Balanus glandula and Balanus trigonus or the bryozoans Bugulina stolonifera and Schizoporella errata exhibit functional attributes that allow them to colonise and spread in new areas. This combined biogeographic and functional approach may contribute to clarify the role of aquaculture on the transport of NIS and to predict and prevent their spreading worldwide. © 2021 Elsevier Ltd},
	author_keywords = {Biogeographic distribution; Fouling organisms; Functional traits; Invasive species; Mytilus galloprovincialis; Offshore aquaculture},
