	affiliations = {School of Marine Ecology and Environment, Shanghai Ocean University, Shanghai, 201306, China; Shanghai Water Environment Ecological University Engineering Research Center, Shanghai, 201306, China},
	abstract = {The increase in global ocean temperature over the last few decades has had a massive impact on the survival and distribution of seagrasses in coastal zones. Zostera marina, a representative species of seagrass ecosystems in China, is subject to intensive global warming. However, the effects of water temperature on the growth and photosynthetic physiology of Z. marina remain unclear. This study assessed the adaptability of Z. marina to different temperatures by examining: (a) the growth indices (fresh weight, leaf number, leaf length, root number, and root length), (b) chlorophyll fluorescence parameters (maximum photochemical efficiency, F<sub>v</sub>/F<sub>m</sub>; actual photosynthetic efficiency, Y(II); photochemical fluorescence quenching, q<sub>P</sub>; non-photochemical quenching coefficent, NPQ), and (c) photosynthetic pigment content (chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, chlorophyll a + b, and carotenoids) under five different temperatures (10, 15, 20, 25, and 30 ℃). (a) In the 10 and 15 ℃ groups, the growth indices of Z. marina showed an increasing trend. At 20 ℃, the fresh weight increased significantly, and the root number decreased significantly (P < 0.05). All the growth indices decreased at 25 ℃ and 30 ℃ (P < 0.05); (b) The chlorophyll fluorescence parameter F<sub>v</sub>/F<sub>m</sub> decreased significantly in the 10 ℃ group and increased significantly in the 20 ℃ group (P < 0.05). There was no significant change in chlorophyll fluorescence parameters in the 15 ℃, 25 ℃ or 30 ℃ groups. Except for the significant increase in the 20 ℃ group, Y(II) decreased significantly in all groups. q<sub>P</sub> did not change in the 10 ℃ group, but decreased in the 15, 20, 25, and 30 ℃ groups. NPQ increased significantly in the 10-20 ℃ group, showed no significant change in the 25 ℃ group, and decreased in the 30 ℃ group (P < 0.05). (c) The photosynthetic pigment content that was significantly reduced in the 10 ℃ and 30 ℃ groups (P < 0.05) but did not change in the 15 ℃ group. Except for carotenoids, none of the photosynthetic pigment content indices increased significantly in the 20 ℃ group. The 25 ℃ group showed a decrease in all pigments, except for chlorophyll a. The experiments indicate that temperature change has a significant impact on Z. marina. It had a beneficial effect on the growth of Z. marina within the range of 10-20 ℃, while under a prolonged exposure time > 20 ℃, the seagrass gradually decayed and died. These findings provide a scientific basis for the restoration of the damaged seagrass ecosystem and are of great importance in the protection of the seagrass ecosystem in the country. © 2022 Science Press. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Chlorophyll fluorescence; Growth index; Photosynthetic pigment content; Temperature; Zostera marina},
	correspondence_address = {P. He; School of Marine Ecology and Environment, Shanghai Ocean University, Shanghai, 201306, China; email: pmhe@shou.edu.com},
