	doi = {10.1093/plankt/fbz010},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85074916718&doi=10.1093%2Fplankt%2Ffbz010&partnerID=40&md5=6c7d59e1989a17590289847da0b3bfbb},
	affiliations = {Department of Environmental Science and Analytical Chemistry, Stockholm University, Stockholm, SE-10691, Sweden},
	abstract = {The size structure of a zooplankton community is frequently used as a trait reflecting functional properties, including biochemical composition. Therefore, a shift in zooplankton body size can reflect shifts in the nutritional quality of zooplankton. In dominant Baltic copepods and cladocerans, neutral to polar lipid ratio (NL/PL ratio), a proxy for the mass-normalized lipid storage, was determined and related to individual body weight. A significant relationship between the NL/PL ratio and body weight was found; the latter was the strongest and the most significant predictor of the lipid storage capacity across different species and developmental stages. These findings provide support for using mean body weight in zooplankton community as a proxy for lipid storage capacity of zooplankton prey and justify applicability of zooplankton mean size as an indicator of nutritional conditions for Baltic zooplanktivores. © 2019 The Author(s).},
