	affiliations = {Department of Marine Biology, Faculty of Marine Science, Chabahar Maritime and Marine University, Chabahar, Province of Sistan dan Baluchestan, Iran; Department of Fisheries, Chabahar Branch, Islamic Azad Univerdity, Chabahar, Province of Sistan and Baluchestan, Iran; Deputy of Marine Environment, Marine Environment Division, Department of Envirinment, Iran; Young Researchers and Elite Club, Shiraz Branch, Islamic Azad University, Shiraz, Iran},
	abstract = {A first step in marine conservation plans is to identify and classify habitat types. These classifications should be scientificallyrigorous with high application. The Coastal and Marine Ecological Classification Standard (CMECS) is a nested, hierarchical framework that applies a uniform set of rules that have a high capacity in marine habitats classifications. In this study, this capacity was tested for the Abu Musa Island ecosystem, Persian Gulf. The Surface Geology Component and the Biotic CoverComponent were analyzed and encoded with the given instructions. In total, 21 codes were determined for benthic communities and 19 codes for geoform component, which showed high diversity of habitats and variety of biotopes in this island.Finally, results indicated that the CMECS approach was successful in Abu Musa habitat classification. In addition, results were very useful for sustainable development especially for ecotourism plans. © 2016, Society for Indonesian Biodiversity. All rights reserved.},
