	affiliations = {UPMC Univ Paris 06, UMR 7621, Observatoire Océanologique, F-66650, Banyuls/Mer, France; CNRS, UMR 7621, Observatoire Océanologique, F-66650, Banyuls/Mer, France; Pierre Fabre Dermocosmétique, F-31035 Toulouse, France; Institut de Recherche Pierre Fabre, F-31035 Toulouse, France},
	abstract = {A novel chemoorganotrophic, non spore forming bacterium with a cell size of 2.3 ± 0.3 /im long and 1.0 ± 0.1 μm wide was isolated from aquifer water in the Cévennes mountains, France. Cells of the isolate, designated strain LMB64<sup>T</sup>, were motile, straight rods with a single polar flagellum, and are able to accumulate poly-/J-hydroxybutyrate granules. Growth was observed at temperatures between 14 and 45 <sup>0</sup>C (optimum 30 <sup>0</sup>C) and pH between 6-0 and 8-0 (optimum pH = 6-0). NaCl was not required for growth. Catalase, oxidase and arginine dihydrolase activities and indole production were detected. The major cellular fatty acids were C <sub>16.0</sub> and C<sub>16.1</sub>ω7c or/and C<sub>16.1</sub>ω6c (40.1 and 36.2%, respectively). The predominant respiratory quinone was ubiquinone 8 (Q-8), but another dominant compound, which did not elute with Q8 or any documented quinone, was observed. The G+C content of the genomic DNA was 64.2 mol %. A phylogenetic analysis based on 16S rRNA gene sequences indicated that the strain LMB64<sup>T</sup> forms a distinct lineage within the family Neisseriaceae of the β-Proteobacteria class. The strain LMB64<sup>T</sup> appears closely related to Pseudogulbenkiania subflava<sup>T</sup> (95.7% 16S rRNA gene sequence similarity), Gulbenkiania mobilis<sup>T</sup> (94.7%) and Paludibacterium yongneupense<sup>T</sup> (94.6%). On the basis of its physiological and molecular properties, the strain LMB64<sup>T</sup>(= CNCM 1-4290) is proposed as the type strain of Aquaphilus dolomiae gen. nov., sp. nov.},
