	title = {Use of copepods in marine ecotoxicology: Tigriopus brevicornis as an example; [Utilisation des copépodes en écotoxicologie marine : L'exemple de Tigriopus brevicornis]},
	year = {1999},
	journal = {Oceanis},
	volume = {25},
	number = {4},
	pages = {609 - 650},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0033320842&partnerID=40&md5=381b70f03007ef2dcde4905d4c00f272},
	affiliations = {Lab. physiol. etres marins GDR CNRS, Institut océanographique, Paris 75005, 195, rue Saint-Jacques, France},
	abstract = {This papers presents a general overview of research in fundamental and applied ecotoxicology carried out at the Institut océanographique, Paris, over the past fifteen years. The studies use as test organism a harpacticoid copepod of the genus Tigriopus which has a worldwide distribution and of which the species Tigriopus brevicornis is abundant off the Atlantic coasts of Europe, and to a lesser degree along the shores of the Mediterranean (Bozic, 1960). This species typically lives in rockpools found at the limits of the midlittoral and supralittoral levels. These rock-pools represent natural mesocosms which can emerge during long periods preceeding large tides, and which are exposed to high amplitude variations in aphotic factors (temperature, salinity, etc.). Tigriopus brevicornis is well adapted to these conditions and live symbiotically with the enteromorphic green algae: Enteromorpha intestinalis. Available throughout the year the copepod is a test organism which can be used and preserved relatively easily under laboratory conditions. Following the presentation of the species and of the various types of tests (lethal and sublethal), the authors discuss the studies undertaken: - research on biomarkers: - of an enzymatic nature (LAP, PGM, MDH and APH), which are sensitive to the metals under study (Cu, Zn, Ni, Cd, Ag and Hg); sensitivity of acetylcholinesterase (involved in neurotransmission of the nervous influx) to pesticides and herbicides, - of metallothionein types sensitive to metals and active in detoxification and storage of contaminants; - research on toxic effects and bioaccumulation. The results obtained have led to applications in environmental research: determination of the toxicity of natural sediments, impact of dredged muds and organic contaminants (herbicids, pesticides, hydrocarbons) on Tigriopus brevicornis.},
	author_keywords = {Biomarkers; Brevicornis; Copepod; Marine ecotoxicology; Tigriopus},
	correspondence_address = {J.-F. Pavillon; Lab. physiol. etres marins GDR CNRS, Institut océanographique, Paris 75005, 195, rue Saint-Jacques, France; email: jf.pavillon@oceano.org},
