	affiliations = {Depto. de Biología Vegetal, Facultad de Biología, Univ. de Santiago de Compostela, E-15071 Santiago de Compostela, Spain},
	abstract = {The process of colonization and succession of a Fucus vesiculosus community has been studied monthly by scraping and sterilizing twelve natural surfaces. The research was carried out over a period of 20-23 months in two localities in Galicia (northwestern Iberian Peninsula), one of them subjected to industrial pollution. After a fast initial colonization by diatoms, Cyanophyceae and Chlorophyceae, and a subsequent prevalence of the latter, the Fucus vesiculosus community reached its climax after one year (sometimes before, depending on the time of sterilization), with the predominance of Fucus vesiculosus in the polluted locality. We attribute this phenomenon to the physicochemical environmental conditions, as well as the industrial pollution produced by the paper mill and the chlorine-soda factories. In the reference locality the process is different and the climax community was not reached at the end of the period of study.},
